  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "ed60a8b5-f289-43f4-b02d-d6d39da32f1e",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Determining relationship between two assets - establishing simple pairs trading strategy"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "dda219cf-71d5-45de-add0-eee49028cfe2",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "One effective method in which we can determine whether there is meaningful  error correcting relationship in the long run is the VECM representation framework of Granger-Johansen. This works by stacking the process into a cointegrated VAR(1) system. From there we rewrite this in terms of first differences, which gives the full VECM representation. $\\gamma$ is then derived from this long-run equilibrium relationship. This full derivation is illustrated below."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "57d286b5-b4d3-45cc-a1f6-e8ba32148f71",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## The Vector Error Correction Model (VECM) Representation\n",
    "\n",
    "To formally analyze the long-run equilibrium and short-run dynamic adjustments between Coca-Cola ($\\ln(\\text{KO}_t)$) and Pepsi ($\\ln(\\text{PEP}_t)$), the Granger-Johansen Vector Error Correction Model (VECM) framework can be utilized.\n",
    "\n",
    "We begin by defining our $K$-dimensional vector of endogenous variables ($K=2$) containing our log prices:\n",
    "$$Y_t = \\begin{bmatrix} \\ln(\\text{KO}_t) \\\\ \\ln(\\text{PEP}_t) \\end{bmatrix}$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Assuming $Y_t$ follows a Vector Autoregressive process of order $p$, a $\\text{VAR}(p)$ system can be written as:\n",
    "$$Y_t = \\nu + \\sum_{i=1}^{p} A_i Y_{t-i} + u_t$$\n",
    "\n",
    "where $\\nu$ is a vector of deterministic constants and $u_t \\sim \\text{w.u.n.}(\\mathbf{0}, \\Sigma)$ represents white noise innovations. Following Granger's Representation Theorem, if the variables in $Y_t$ are integrated of order one, $I(1)$ (they can be differenced once to make them weakly stationary process (i.e. to make them $I(0)$) and if they are cointegrated with a cointegrating rank $r$, the system can be rewritten in its first-difference VECM representation (setting our lag length in first-differences to $k_{ar\\_diff} = p-1 = 1$):\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\\Delta Y_t = \\nu + \\Pi Y_{t-1} + \\Gamma_1 \\Delta Y_{t-1} + u_t$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Where:\n",
    "* $\\Delta Y_t = Y_t - Y_{t-1} = \\begin{bmatrix} \\Delta \\ln(\\text{KO}_t) \\\\ \\Delta \\ln(\\text{PEP}_t) \\end{bmatrix}$ is the vector of continuously compounded returns.\n",
    "* $\\Gamma_1 = -(A_2 + A_3 + \\dots + A_p)$ captures the short-run transient dynamics of the system.\n",
    "* $\\Pi = \\sum_{i=1}^{p} A_i - I_K$ is the long-run impact matrix that contains the cointegrating structural parameters.\n",
    "\n",
    "#### Decomposition of the Impact Matrix $\\Pi$ and the Error Correction Mechanism\n",
    "\n",
    "If the system is cointegrated with a rank deficiency condition of $\\text{rank}(\\Pi) = r = 1$, the matrix $\\Pi$ can be decomposed into the product of two distinct $2 \\times 1$ matrices, $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$:\n",
    "$$\\Pi = \\alpha \\beta'$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Substituting this decomposition back into our VECM representation yields:\n",
    "$$\\Delta Y_t = \\nu + \\alpha \\left( \\beta' Y_{t-1} \\right) + \\Gamma_1 \\Delta Y_{t-1} + u_t$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Expanding this system explicitly into our structural parameters allows us to parse out the individual equations:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\\begin{bmatrix} \\Delta \\ln(\\text{KO}_t) \\\\ \\Delta \\ln(\\text{PEP}_t) \\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix} \\nu_1 \\\\ \\nu_2 \\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix} \\alpha_1 \\\\ \\alpha_2 \\end{bmatrix} \\left( \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & \\gamma \\end{bmatrix} \\begin{bmatrix} \\ln(\\text{KO}_{t-1}) \\\\ \\ln(\\text{PEP}_{t-1}) \\end{bmatrix} \\right) + \\Gamma_1 \\begin{bmatrix} \\Delta \\ln(\\text{KO}_{t-1}) \\\\ \\Delta \\ln(\\text{PEP}_{t-1}) \\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix} u_{1,t} \\\\ u_{2,t} \\end{bmatrix}$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Where:\n",
    "1. **The Cointegrating Vector ($\\beta'$):** $$\\beta' = \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & \\gamma \\end{bmatrix}$$\n",
    "   This vector represents the long-run equilibrium relationship. Normalized with respect to KO, the term inside the parenthesis defines our stationary long-run residual spread: \n",
    "   $$\\text{s}_{t-1} = \\beta' Y_{t-1} = \\ln(\\text{KO}_{t-1}) + \\gamma \\ln(\\text{PEP}_{t-1})$$\n",
    "   (Note: Aligns with later empirical Johansen estimation, $\\gamma \\approx -0.7924$, matching the structural spread formulation $\\ln(\\text{KO}) - 0.7924\\ln(\\text{PEP})$).\n",
    "\n",
    "2. **The Speed of Adjustment Vector ($\\alpha$):**\n",
    "   $$\\alpha = \\begin{bmatrix} \\alpha_1 \\\\ \\alpha_2 \\end{bmatrix}$$\n",
    "   The coefficients $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_2$ dictate the error-correcting feedback mechanism (the speed of adjustment). They measure how fast each asset's return reacts to clear out historical deviations from the long-run equilibrium spread. For the system to mean-revert stably, $\\alpha_1$ must be negative, dragging the price of KO downward when the spread is positively overextended."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "f82c5e3e-b52f-4e07-a76f-4279785326ea",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The residual spread at time t is given by $s_t = \\beta'Y_t = ln(KO_t)$ + $\\gamma ln(PEP_t)$ where $\\gamma$ is the hedging ratio."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "b57b994d-e157-4e14-80a9-d21a29169bb9",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Johansen (1998) - Trace and Maximum Eigenvalue Tests"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "e1d3a922-67e8-49ce-9298-c7061abb205f",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "There are two main statistical tests available to hypothesis test the exact number of cointegrating relations present - the trace and max eigenvalues tests of Johansen (1998).\n",
    "#### Trace test ####\n",
    "Trace test uses the following test statistic:\n",
    "#### $-T\\cdot\\Sigma_{i=r+1}^{n} \\ln(1-\\lambda_{i})$\n",
    "where $T$ is the sample size, $\\lambda_i$ is eigenvalues of estimated matrix of cointegrating row vectors and $r$ is the number of cointegrating relations. The null hypothesis of the trace test is that there is a number of cointegrating relations that is less than or equal to a given number $r$. This hypothesis test is applied iteratively for different values of $r$ until we fail to reject the null hypothesis."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 39,
   "id": "627f579e-06c7-48e4-886d-c2ce14b5123b",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "--- JOHANSEN TRACE TEST RESULTS ---\n",
      "Rank | Trace Stat | Critical Values (90%, 95%, 99%)\n",
      "-------------------------------------------------------\n",
      "r <= 0 | 15.3833     | [13.4294 15.4943 19.9349]\n",
      "r <= 1 | 0.5060     | [2.7055 3.8415 6.6349]\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "johansen_result = coint_johansen(log_prices_train[[\"KO\", \"PEP\"]], det_order=0, k_ar_diff=1) # Johansen tests of trace & max eigenvalue\n",
    "# det_order = 0 gives constant term (intercept), k_ar_diff = 1 is the number of lags we have chosen to rewrite in terms of first differences\n",
    "# Print Trace test result for number of cointegrating relations\n",
    "print(\"--- JOHANSEN TRACE TEST RESULTS ---\")\n",
    "print(\"Rank | Trace Stat | Critical Values (90%, 95%, 99%)\")\n",
    "print(\"-\" * 55)\n",
    "for i in range(len(johansen_result.lr1)):\n",
    "    print(f\"r <= {i} | {johansen_result.lr1[i]:.4f}     | {johansen_result.cvt[i]}\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "d6ce2845-bbb2-4c20-a796-b1d6c11deaeb",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The trace test for rank <= 0 has a trace test statistic that is significant at the 95% confidence level, meaning we reject the null of no cointegrating relation (at 5% significance level).\n",
    "The trace test for rank <= 1 has a trace test statistic that is not statistically significant, so we fail to reject the null of one cointegrating relation.\n",
    "\n",
    "Thus, the implication from the trace test is that there is at most one cointegrating relation (with 95% confidence)."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "1c686df2-4350-4cf8-a679-d9a3dfe8b6ff",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "#### Max eigenvalues test"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "3b9ceee6-54cf-405c-bf18-9034a0f37b7b",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The maximum eigenvalue test uses following test statistic:\n",
    "\n",
    "$-T \\cdot ln(1-\\lambda_{r+1})$, \n",
    "\n",
    "where $T$ is sample size and $\\lambda_{r+1}$ is the $r+1^{th}$ largest eigenvalue when eigenvalues are ordered from smallest to largest. The test evaluates a null hypothesis that the number of cointegrating relations is exactly equal to $r$ against an alternative hypothesis of $r+1$ cointegrating relations. Similar to the trace test, this hypothesis test is applied iteratively, i.e. for r = 0 vs r = 1, then for r = 1 vs r = 2 etc. until we fail to reject the null hypothesis."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 43,
   "id": "5208b76f-4794-4387-b50a-da28c7219b04",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "--- JOHANSEN MAXIMUM EIGENVALUE TEST RESULTS ---\n",
      "Hypothesis Test | Max Eigenvalue Stat | Critical Values (90%, 95%, 99%)\n",
      "-------------------------------------------------------\n",
      "r = 0 vs r = 1 | 14.8772     | [12.2971 14.2639 18.52  ]\n",
      "r = 1 vs r = 2 | 0.5060     | [2.7055 3.8415 6.6349]\n",
      "=======================================================\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "print(\"--- JOHANSEN MAXIMUM EIGENVALUE TEST RESULTS ---\")\n",
    "print(\"Hypothesis Test | Max Eigenvalue Stat | Critical Values (90%, 95%, 99%)\")\n",
    "print(\"-\" * 55)\n",
    "for i in range(len(johansen_result.max_eig_stat)):\n",
    "    print(f\"r = {i} vs r = {i+1} | {johansen_result.max_eig_stat[i]:.4f}     | {johansen_result.max_eig_stat_crit_vals[i]}\")\n",
    "print(\"=\"*55)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "566288c1-e715-447b-bdf5-abee40787c26",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The maximum eigenvalue test of null of no cointegration relations against alternative of one cointegration relation has a test statistic that is significant at 95% confidence level. So we reject the null of no cointegrating relations.\n",
    "\n",
    "The maximum eigenvalue test of null of one cointegrating relation against alternative of two cointegrating relations has a test statistic that is not statistically significant, so we fail to reject null of one cointegrating relation.\n",
    "\n",
    "The implication from the maximum eigenvalue test is thus also that there is at most one cointegrating relation (with 95% confidence). Both tests happen to agree in this case and imply there is exactly one cointegrating relation."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 45,
   "id": "c79e4aab-af26-49a8-bd1b-239ab1e65ae5",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "vecm_result = VECM(endog=log_prices_train[[\"KO\", \"PEP\"]], k_ar_diff=1, coint_rank=1, deterministic = 'ci').fit() # Fit the entire VECM representation\n",
    "# coint_rank = 1 is the rank of the cointegrating matrix when we fit the VECM since we just found one cointegrating relation "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
